Bacterial Enteritis (Campylobacter jejuni)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Prodromal fever/myalgia, Crampy periumbilical pain, Diarrhea progressing to bloody stools

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-007
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, crampy abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea with signs of dehydration, requiring urgent after-hours evaluation to prevent severe volume depletion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a progression from watery to frankly bloody diarrhea, accompanied by fever and an inability to maintain oral hydration due to nausea. This presentation necessitated an urgent clinical assessment to evaluate hemodynamic stability and address moderate dehydration that could not safely wait until the next routine in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Bloody diarrhea, fever, and severe stomach cramps. Patient states, The stomach cramps are doubling me over, and now there is blood every time I run to the toilet.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced 48 hours ago with a prodrome of fever, headache, and myalgias. This was followed yesterday by an acute onset of crampy periumbilical abdominal pain and frequent watery diarrhea. Over the past 12 hours, the diarrhea rapidly progressed to bloody stools with significant tenesmus. The patient reports nausea without active vomiting, but fluid intake has been poor. The patient feels lightheaded upon standing and has noticed decreased urination, indicating evolving dehydration.
Functional Status: Independent but confined to bed and bathroom due to weakness and frequent bowel movements.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of unobserved syncope secondary to volume loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 38.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68.0 Kg
General: Appears fatigued, flushed, and is guarding the abdomen when coughing or moving.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia with regular rhythm, normal heart sounds. Delayed capillary refill of 3 seconds. Postural drop in blood pressure evident. Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is non-distended but exhibits moderate tenderness, particularly in the periumbilical region and the right lower quadrant, mimicking appendicitis. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. There is no rebound tenderness or rigidity. Integumentary: The diagnostic triad is supported by the history of prodromal fever/myalgia, current crampy periumbilical pain, and diarrhea progressing to bloody stools. Examination confirms decreased skin turgor and very dry oral mucous membranes, indicating moderate gastrointestinal fluid loss.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Adequate access to a private bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent antibiotic use or chronic immunosuppressive therapy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bacterial Enteritis (Campylobacter jejuni) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of massive, frequent bloody diarrhea and a prodrome of watery diarrhea, which are highly atypical for appendicitis despite the right lower quadrant pain.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No prior history of IBD, and the distinct acute infectious prodrome (fever, myalgias) preceding the gastrointestinal symptoms.

Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: High fever, severe right lower quadrant pain, and the definitive progression to gross bloody stools with tenesmus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The clinical picture of a febrile prodrome progressing to crampy abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea strongly suggests an invasive bacterial enteritis, highly characteristic of Campylobacter jejuni. The patient exhibits objective signs of moderate dehydration, including tachycardia, postural hypotension, and dry mucous membranes. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a high risk for progression to severe hypovolemia or acute kidney injury due to uncompensated fluid loss. Urgent after-hours assessment was necessary to exclude a surgical abdomen (e.g., appendicitis), confirm hemodynamic stability, and initiate an aggressive, structured oral rehydration regimen. The expected investigation results include curved, comma-shaped or seagull-winged Gram-negative bacilli on stool microscopy and positive stool culture on Skirrow agar that would assist in confirming the diagnosis via routine outpatient pathology.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to commence strict oral rehydration therapy. Prescribed Paracetamol 1000mg PO PRN for fever and myalgia. Strongly advised against the use of antimotility agents (e.g., Loperamide) as they can prolong the illness and increase complication risks.
Positive Behavioral Change: To effectively replace the fluids you are losing, commit to drinking 200ml of an oral rehydration solution specifically after every loose bowel movement. Set up a physical tally sheet in the bathroom; making a tally mark after finishing each glass will provide visual proof of your active recovery. The direct reduction in your dizziness and fatigue will reinforce this hydration habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 48 hours for clinical review and to arrange formal stool microscopy and culture.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department or call 000 if your abdominal pain becomes constant, severe, and board-like, if you are unable to keep any fluids down for more than 4 hours, or if you feel entirely unable to stand without fainting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical presentation; precise bacterial identification requires stool culture.
Clinical Course: This infection is typically self-limiting, peaking at 3-5 days. Maintaining aggressive hydration is the primary treatment to prevent complications while the immune system clears the bacteria.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not take any anti-diarrhea medications from the pharmacy, as trapping the bacteria in your bowel can lead to severe toxic complications.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bowel is weeping fluid and blood due to a bacterial infection. Plain water is insufficient; oral rehydration salts are required to replace the specific electrolytes being lost in the diarrhea.
Actionable Steps: Drink 200ml of rehydration fluid after every loose stool. Track your intake visually to ensure you meet the required volume.

